# 10. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE

Use Supabase PostgreSQL.

Create a normalized relational database.

Recommended tables:

## 10.1 tasks

Stores task information.

## 10.2 categories

Stores user-specific categories.

## 10.3 tags

Stores user-specific tags.

## 10.4 task_tags

Maps tasks to multiple tags.

## 10.5 subtasks

Stores child tasks.

## 10.6 recurring_rules

Stores recurrence configuration.

## 10.7 focus_sessions

Stores focus and Pomodoro history.

## 10.8 activity_logs

Stores task-related activities.

## 10.9 user_preferences

Stores interface and productivity settings.

Additional tables may be introduced only when justified by the actual data model.

### Database requirements

- UUID primary keys.
- Foreign key constraints.
- Appropriate indexes.
- NOT NULL constraints.
- Check constraints.
- Ownership relationships.
- Timestamps.
- Appropriate deletion behavior.
- Referential integrity.
- Efficient queries.

Use database migrations or organized SQL schema files.

Do not rely on manually creating tables without preserving the schema in the project.

## 10.10 Important relationships

- One user can own multiple tasks.
- One user can own multiple categories.
- One user can own multiple tags.
- One category can contain multiple tasks.
- One task can have multiple subtasks.
- One task can have multiple tags.
- One user can have multiple focus sessions.
- One task can have multiple activity records.

Allow tasks without categories.

Use a single primary category per task.

Completed tasks remain editable.

Archived tasks remain restorable.

Deleted tasks use soft deletion until permanent removal.

Subtasks must not be accessible independently of their parent task's ownership.

